<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hunald I of Aquitaine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 735 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Hunald</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hunald was Duke of Aquitaine from 735, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-the-great">Odo the Great</a>. He resisted the authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> and his sons Carloman and Pippin the Short, who campaigned against him repeatedly. In 745 he gave up power to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waiofar">Waiofar</a> and retired to a monastery on the Île de Ré.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hunald-i-of-aquitaine.jpg" alt="Hunald I of Aquitaine" width="284" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Master of the City of Ladies · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hunald.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hunald, also spelled Hunold, Hunoald or Chunoald, inherited an Aquitaine that his father had enlarged with lands along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, once Neustrian, and the Auvergne, once Austrasian. Nominally an officer of the Merovingian kings, he in practice ruled an autonomous duchy at a time when the mayors of the palace held real power. His brother Hatto seems at first to have shared his rule. In 735 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> marched across Aquitaine and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, and the brothers gave a promise of faith to him and his sons and agreed to remit taxes, after which Charles left the duchy to Hunald.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The brothers rebelled in 736. Hatto was captured and later escaped, and Hunald then invited him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, blinded him and shut him in a monastery, probably the price Charles demanded for leaving him his duchy. During Charles's campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimania">Septimania</a> and Provence in 736–39, Hunald may have harassed his forces. After Charles died in 741, Hunald rose again in 742 with the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and Alemannia. Carloman and Pippin crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, burned the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourges">Bourges</a> and razed Loches; once they had gone, Hunald crossed the Loire himself and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a>, where he is said to have burned the church of Saint Mary, the earliest mention of the later cathedral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When the brothers invaded again in 745, Hunald swore to obey them, gave hostages and paid the taxes owed, then took a monk's vow and handed power to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waiofar">Waiofar</a>. About 752 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and joined a monastery attached to Saint Peter's Basilica. He died there in 756 when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aistulf">Aistulf</a> attacked the city, probably leading the defence of Saint Peter's, although he may have been stoned to death. Some historians believed he returned to lead Aquitaine in 768, but that leader was probably a different Hunald, his grandson. He probably inspired the hero of the twelfth-century chanson de geste Huon de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hunald I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Odo's son <b>Hunald</b> and grandson Waiofar resisted the Carolingians, but Pippin the Short waged yearly wars against Waiofar until his murder in 768.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-duchy-of-aquitaine">Early Duchy of Aquitaine</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Pavia, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.19,9.16&amp;year=735" aria-label="Pavia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4768 569 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Hunald I of Aquitaine"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 839h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 839h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=45.19,9.16&amp;year=735">What was here in 735 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
